<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="43352" href="schedule/2022/spring/ENGL/504/43352.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2022" href="schedule/2022.xml">2022</calendarYear><term id="120221" href="schedule/2022/spring.xml">Spring 2022</term><subject id="ENGL" href="schedule/2022/spring/ENGL.xml">English</subject><course id="504" href="schedule/2022/spring/ENGL/504.xml">Theories of Cinema</course></parents><sectionNumber>A  </sectionNumber><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This semester the course begins with a review of basic and formative film theory, understood within the historical context in which it was and is written and received.  Building on this groundwork, the course then moves on to consider rhetorical aspects of film theory and asks what theories film scholars can use to address the relationships among film, politics, and society.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2022-01-18Z</startDate><endDate>2022-05-04Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LAB">Laboratory</type><start>04:00 PM</start><end>06:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>T      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>115</roomNumber><buildingName>Spurlock Museum</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Oyallon-Koloski" firstName="J">Oyallon-Koloski, J</instructor></instructors></meeting><meeting id="1"><type code="LEC">Lecture</type><start>02:30 PM</start><end>03:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>T      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>1112</roomNumber><buildingName>Foreign Languages Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Oyallon-Koloski" firstName="J">Oyallon-Koloski, J</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>